\paragraph{Fleiss' $\kappa$ (surface, instance-level).}
We adapt the inter-rater agreement measure of \citet{fleiss1971agreement} by treating the $N$ samples as independent annotators, token positions as items, and the token vocabulary as the set of categories:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fk}
\kappa = \frac{\bar{P} - \bar{P}_e}{1 - \bar{P}_e}
\end{equation}
where $\bar{P}$ is the observed agreement rate across token positions and $\bar{P}_e$ is the agreement expected by chance.
Unlike EM, FK captures partial agreement---two samples that share most tokens but differ in a few positions receive a high score.
However, FK operates at the string level and does not account for the structure of the extracted output.

tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{Fleiss' $\kappa$ (surface, instance-level).}
We adapt the inter-rater agreement measure of \citet{fleiss1971agreement} to operate on extracted entity sets.
Unlike the classical token-level formulation, our items are the set of unique (text, type) entity tuples observed across all $N$ samples, and the $N$ samples serve as annotators who each assign a binary label---\textit{present} or \textit{absent}---to every entity, yielding a $|E_\cup| \times 2$ rating matrix:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fk}
\kappa = \frac{\bar{P} - \bar{P}_e}{1 - \bar{P}_e}
\end{equation}
where $\bar{P}$ is the mean observed pairwise agreement rate across entities and $\bar{P}_e$ is the expected agreement under random assignment with the same marginal frequencies.
This entity-level binary formulation captures partial overlap between extraction sets: two samples sharing most entities but disagreeing on a few receive a moderately high $\kappa$.
Because it relies on exact string matching of entity mentions, FK does not tolerate near-synonym variations (e.g., ``machine learning'' vs.\ ``ML''), which the structural signals handle through soft span matching.

tool_args.old_string:
The pairwise Soft Jaccard score is:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sj}
\text{SJ}(y_i, y_j) = \frac{|M|}{\max(|A_i|, |A_j|)}
\end{equation}
where $|M|$ is the number of matched extraction pairs and $|A_i|$, $|A_j|$ are the extraction counts of each sample.

tool_args.new_string:
The pairwise Soft Jaccard score is:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sj}
\text{SJ}(y_i, y_j) = \frac{\sum_{m \in M} \text{sim}(a_m, b_m)}{\sum_{g} \max(|g_i|, |g_j|)}
\end{equation}
where $M$ is the set of matched extraction pairs from the Hungarian algorithm, $\text{sim}(a_m, b_m)$ is the span-level Jaccard overlap between matched extractions, and $g_i$, $g_j$ index the extraction counts of samples $y_i$ and $y_j$ within entity type group $g$.
The numerator sums soft similarities rather than counting matches, allowing partial credit for near-overlapping spans.
The denominator uses $\max$ per type group rather than set union, which more heavily penalizes hallucinated extractions that inflate one sample's extraction count.

